referenceSolutions.python:
def switchboardRingsPeakTicket(links, k):
    a=links
    out=[]
    from collections import deque
    n=len(a)
    dist=[-1]*n
    queue=deque()
    if n:
        dist[0]=0
        queue.append(0)
    while queue:
        u=queue.popleft()
        for v in range(n):
            if a[u][v] and dist[v]==-1:
                dist[v]=dist[u]+1
                queue.append(v)
    out=dist
    return max(out,default=0)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int switchboardRingsPeakTicket(int[][] links, int k) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]dist=new int[n];Arrays.fill(dist,-1);Deque<Integer>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();if(n>0){dist[0]=0;queue.add(0);}while(!queue.isEmpty()){int u=queue.remove();for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]!=0&&dist[v]==-1){dist[v]=dist[u]+1;queue.add(v);}}for(int d:dist)out.add(d);}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.max(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

links is a square 0/1 adjacency matrix of a directed graph. From vertex 0, produce the shortest number of edges to every vertex in index order. Unreachable vertices contribute -1; an empty graph produces no distances.

Return the largest value in the derived sequence, or 0 for an empty sequence. Negative maxima must remain negative.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(V^2); space O(n).

